---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import ConverterPageFrame from '../components/ConverterPageFrame.astro';
import FaqList from '../components/FaqList.astro';
import SubtitlePremiumStyles from '../components/SubtitlePremiumStyles.astro';
import { SUBTITLE_CONVERTER, SUBTITLE_PAIR_CONVERTERS } from '../config/subtitle-converters';
import { SUBTITLE_FAQ } from '../content/subtitle-faq';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';
const converter = SUBTITLE_CONVERTER;
const schema = {'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'WebApplication',name:'ConvertAnyFile Subtitle Converter',url:`${SITE.origin}/subtitle-converter/`,applicationCategory:'UtilitiesApplication',operatingSystem:'Any',offers:{'@type':'Offer',price:'0',priceCurrency:'USD'},featureList:['SRT, VTT/WebVTT, ASS, SSA and SBV input','SRT, VTT, SBV and TXT output','Full-file local preflight analysis','Exact millisecond timing shift','Formatting cleanup','Flexible transcript export','Batch conversion','Line ending and BOM controls','No file upload']};
---
<SiteLayout title={converter.title} description={converter.description} schema={schema}>
<SubtitlePremiumStyles />
<ConverterPageFrame converter={converter} introSuffix=" Preview files are fully preflight-checked in a local worker before they become Ready; keep important source subtitles until you verify the converted copy.">
  <div slot="after-tool" class="converter-info-grid"><div class="converter-info-card"><strong>5 input families</strong><span>SRT, WebVTT, ASS, SSA, and SBV timed text.</span></div><div class="converter-info-card"><strong>17 real pair routes</strong><span>Every useful input → SRT/VTT/SBV/TXT combination is backed by the engine.</span></div><div class="converter-info-card"><strong>Full-file preflight</strong><span>Cue count, timeline, encoding, and richer metadata are checked before Ready.</span></div><div class="converter-info-card"><strong>Advanced tools</strong><span>Exact timing offset, plain-text cleanup, transcript modes, LF/CRLF, and BOM controls.</span></div></div>
</ConverterPageFrame>

<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><div class="content-intro-grid"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Premium Preview</span><h2>More than “change the extension and download.”</h2><p>The Subtitle engine now treats conversion as a timed-text workflow: it validates the complete file in a worker, exposes useful queue metadata, supports exact timing correction, lets you deliberately clean formatting, and offers flexible transcript export without sending the subtitle to a remote conversion service.</p></div><div class="content-summary-card"><strong>Compatibility copy, not fake fidelity</strong><p>SRT, VTT, SBV, ASS, and SSA do not share the same presentation model. Rich metadata is detected before conversion and loss is stated explicitly instead of silently pretending everything survived.</p></div></div><div class="subtitle-premium-strip"><div class="subtitle-premium-item"><strong>Preflight analysis</strong><span>Parse every cue before admission, not just the first few lines.</span></div><div class="subtitle-premium-item"><strong>Exact timing correction</strong><span>Enter any offset from -600000 to +600000 ms, including +1350 or -780.</span></div><div class="subtitle-premium-item"><strong>Formatting cleanup</strong><span>Keep inline markup or intentionally export plain readable cue text.</span></div><div class="subtitle-premium-item"><strong>Transcript export</strong><span>TXT supports no time, start time, or complete start → end ranges.</span></div></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Preflight</span><h2>The queue says Ready only after the whole subtitle parses.</h2><p>Filename checks alone are not enough for subtitle files. Admission runs in the same local Web Worker used for conversion and parses the complete file before accepting it.</p></div><div class="comparison-table-wrap"><table class="comparison-table"><thead><tr><th>Preflight check</th><th>What the user gets</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Complete cue parse</strong></td><td>Broken timing near the end of a file is caught before conversion starts.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SRT structure guard</strong></td><td>Multiple timestamp lines inside one cue block are rejected instead of becoming silent subtitle text.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cue count</strong></td><td>The queue metadata shows how many timed cues were detected.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Timeline end</strong></td><td>A quick duration sanity check appears beside the source format.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Text encoding</strong></td><td>UTF-8, UTF-16LE, UTF-16BE, or Windows-1252 fallback is reported.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rich metadata flag</strong></td><td>ASS/SSA formatting and WebVTT metadata are surfaced before simpler output is chosen.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Advanced controls</span><h2>Useful subtitle transforms, still simple enough for a converter.</h2><p>The goal is not to recreate a full subtitle editor. The controls focus on common conversion problems that are safe to apply consistently across a batch.</p></div><div class="comparison-table-wrap"><table class="comparison-table"><thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Behavior</th><th>Safety rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Timing shift</strong></td><td>Enter an exact -600000 to +600000 millisecond offset.</td><td>A negative shift that would put any cue before 00:00 fails instead of clipping silently; out-of-range values fail clearly.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Plain text only</strong></td><td>Remove inline cue markup from SRT/VTT/SBV output.</td><td>Off by default so normal timed-text formatting is not unexpectedly stripped.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>TXT timestamps</strong></td><td>Choose none, start time, or start → end range.</td><td>TXT always removes markup because it is a transcript-like output.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cue spacing</strong></td><td>Blank-line or single-line separation for TXT.</td><td>Source cue order is never reordered or merged.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>LF / CRLF + BOM</strong></td><td>Control output bytes for downstream tools.</td><td>Output text is always UTF-8.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="content-intro-grid"><div><span class="section-kicker">WebVTT fidelity</span><h2>Same-format VTT cleanup no longer throws away document metadata by default.</h2><p>When a VTT file is exported as VTT without a timing shift or formatting cleanup, the converter validates the document but preserves the original WebVTT body, including document-level STYLE, REGION, NOTE, X-TIMESTAMP-MAP, cue IDs, and cue settings. Line endings and BOM can still be normalized.</p></div><div class="content-summary-card"><strong>Transforms change the contract</strong><p>If timing or text is transformed, the file is intentionally rebuilt from parsed cues. At that point richer document-level VTT metadata cannot be guaranteed and the source should be kept.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Format fidelity</span><h2>What survives depends on the destination.</h2></div><ul class="content-checklist"><li>SRT cue numbers are regenerated deterministically while cue order and timing are kept.</li><li>WebVTT cue identifiers and settings can be represented in VTT, but simpler outputs cannot carry VTT regions or STYLE blocks.</li><li>ASS/SSA Dialogue timing and readable text are extracted while styles, karaoke, positioning, effects, drawing commands, and override tags are removed in simpler outputs.</li><li>SBV timestamps are converted without reordering cues.</li><li>TXT is always plain readable text and can optionally carry timing context.</li></ul></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="content-intro-grid"><div><span class="section-kicker">Encoding</span><h2>Handle common subtitle text without hiding what was detected.</h2><p>The Preview recognizes UTF-8, UTF-16LE, and UTF-16BE byte-order marks. Without a BOM it attempts strict UTF-8 first and then Windows-1252 for common legacy subtitle exports. The detected encoding is shown in queue metadata after full-file preflight.</p></div><div class="content-summary-card"><strong>No filename-only trust</strong><p>Renaming arbitrary text to <code>.srt</code> or <code>.vtt</code> is not enough. The full subtitle structure has to parse before the file becomes Ready.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">17 real conversions</span><h2>Every published pair is backed by the same tested engine.</h2><p>SRT, VTT, ASS, SSA, and SBV are expanded only into destinations that the Preview can really write: SRT, VTT, SBV, and TXT. Same-format SEO pages are intentionally not created.</p></div><div class="related-grid">{SUBTITLE_PAIR_CONVERTERS.map((item)=><a class="related-card" href={`/${item.slug}/`}>{item.eyebrow} <span>→</span></a>)}</div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="content-intro-grid"><div><span class="section-kicker">Preview limits</span><h2>Small text files still get explicit browser safety boundaries.</h2><p>The Preview uses a 5 MB per-file ceiling, 50 MB total batch input, a maximum of 50 files, worker timeouts, a ±600000 ms timing-offset boundary, and a 100,000-cue parser guard. Full-file preflight also prevents partially valid subtitles from entering the ready queue.</p></div><div class="content-summary-card"><strong>What this still does not claim</strong><p>No OCR, speech-to-text, subtitle translation, frame-rate retiming, automatic audio/video synchronization, ASS/SSA style preservation, or video embedding. This remains a deterministic timed-text converter, not a media editor.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><FaqList items={SUBTITLE_FAQ} title="Subtitle conversion questions" intro="Answers about preflight parsing, exact timing correction, SRT, WebVTT metadata, ASS/SSA styling, SBV, transcript export, encodings, local processing, and Preview limits." /></div></section>
</SiteLayout>
